# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# MkDocs copies this file verbatim from docs/ into the build output, and
# Cloudflare Pages reads it from the ROOT of the uploaded directory. That is why
# it lives in docs/ rather than the repo root -- `mkdocs build` only copies
# docs/ into site/, so a root-level _headers would never reach the deploy.
#
# WARNING: Do not add `X-Frame-Options` here. Cloudflare Pages does not set one
# by default, and the only reason this file exists is to make framing explicit.
# An X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN (or DENY) would break
# https://autoboat.aoe.vt.edu/documentation, which renders this site in an
# iframe from a different origin. If you ever need to lock framing down, edit
# the frame-ancestors list below instead.
#
# WARNING: Keep the origin list in step with the site. These are the two hosts
# that serve the website (the www host serves the site directly rather than
# redirecting to the apex, so it is a genuinely different origin).
#
# WARNING: There must be exactly ONE `/*` block. This file originally had two,
# and Pages applied only the second one -- verified against the deployed site:
# x-content-type-options came through but Content-Security-Policy did not.
#
# NOTE: Losing that header did NOT break the iframe, and this is the subtle part.
# With no CSP and no X-Frame-Options, framing is ALLOWED by default, so the page
# kept working. The header is a hardening measure that pins WHO may frame this
# site; it is not what makes framing work. That asymmetry is why the bug was easy
# to miss, and why the note below matters more than it looks.
#
# Add new headers to this block; do not add another `/*` section.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self' https://autoboat.aoe.vt.edu https://www.autoboat.aoe.vt.edu
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# The docs are a public resource; nothing here is secret. Long-lived caching is
# handled by Pages automatically for hashed asset filenames.

